<% content_for :title, "Perspectives on Bipolar & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-perspectives-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-hero">
    <div class="addiction-perspectives-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-perspectives-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION.</span>
        <span>ANOTHER WAY</span>
        <span>TO SEE IT.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-perspectives-intro">
        SAME ADDICTION.<br>
        DIFFERENT FUCKING LENS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-pink">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">01 / THE BIG IDEA</p>

      <h2>
        MORE THAN<br>
        ONE WAY<br>
        IN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Addiction is complicated. Bipolar disorder is complicated.
        Put them together and one explanation usually isn't enough.
      </p>

      <p>
        Biology matters. Mood matters. Habit matters. Reward matters.
        Pain matters. Environment matters. The stories you tell yourself
        matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        Buddhism, Hinduism, Stoicism, CBT, DBT, and IFS don't all
        explain addiction the same way — and they shouldn't.
      </p>

      <p>
        They're different lenses.
      </p>

      <p>
        One might help you understand an urge.
        Another might help you survive it.
        Another might help you understand what the hell you're
        trying to escape in the first place.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU DON'T NEED ONE THEORY<br>
        TO EXPLAIN YOUR WHOLE FUCKING LIFE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 BUDDHISM -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-yellow">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">02 / BUDDHISM</p>

      <h2>
        CRAVING<br>
        ISN'T A<br>
        COMMAND.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Buddhism pays a lot of attention to craving,
        attachment, suffering, and the urge to make discomfort disappear.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's pretty fucking relevant to addiction.
      </p>

      <p>
        An urge can feel like an emergency:
        <strong>I need this to stop. I need to feel different. Now.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        A Buddhist lens creates some space around that experience.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-lens">
        <span>BUDDHISM ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          CAN I EXPERIENCE THIS CRAVING WITHOUT AUTOMATICALLY OBEYING IT?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p>
        The craving appears.
        It gets stronger.
        It changes.
        Eventually, it passes.
      </p>

      <p>
        That doesn't make cravings easy.
        It makes them temporary.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        I WANT IT.<br>
        I DON'T HAVE TO DO IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 HINDUISM -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-purple">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">03 / HINDUISM</p>

      <h2>
        THE URGE<br>
        ISN'T<br>
        ALL OF YOU.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Hindu traditions can offer a way of separating your deeper
        sense of self from the constantly changing activity of the mind.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your mind can scream for alcohol.
        Your mood can crash.
        Your energy can explode.
        Your thoughts can race.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those experiences are real.
      </p>

      <p>
        But they don't have to become your entire identity.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-lens">
        <span>HINDUISM ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          CAN I OBSERVE WHAT MY MIND WANTS WITHOUT CONFUSING IT WITH WHO I AM?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p>
        That distinction matters when addiction starts talking like
        it owns the place.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        MY MIND WANTS SOMETHING.<br>
        I AM MORE THAN WHAT MY MIND WANTS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 STOICISM -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-black">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">04 / STOICISM</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT'S<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        YOURS?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Stoicism draws a hard line between what you control
        and what you don't.
      </p>

      <p>
        You may not control whether a craving appears.
      </p>

      <p>
        You may not control having bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can't rewrite yesterday, erase consequences,
        or force everybody to understand you.
      </p>

      <p>
        But there are things you can influence:
        where you go, who you call, what you keep around you,
        whether you ask for help, whether you leave the situation,
        and what you do next.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-lens">
        <span>STOICISM ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT PART OF THIS MOMENT IS ACTUALLY MINE TO ACT ON?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO CONTROL EVERYTHING.<br>
        YOU NEED THE NEXT USEFUL MOVE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 CBT -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-pink">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">05 / CBT</p>

      <h2>
        CATCH THE<br>
        BULLSHIT<br>
        STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Addiction can come with some extremely convincing
        cognitive bullshit.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-thoughts">
        <p>"ONE WON'T MATTER."</p>
        <p>"I DESERVE THIS."</p>
        <p>"I CAN CONTROL IT THIS TIME."</p>
        <p>"I ALREADY FUCKED UP, SO WHO CARES?"</p>
        <p>"I NEED IT."</p>
        <p>"NOTHING ELSE WILL MAKE ME FEEL BETTER."</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        CBT helps slow that story down and examine it.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-lens">
        <span>CBT ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT AM I TELLING MYSELF — AND DOES IT ACTUALLY HOLD UP?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p>
        The goal isn't positive thinking.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's recognizing when a thought is functioning like
        a permission slip.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        A THOUGHT CAN SOUND CONVINCING<br>
        AND STILL BE FULL OF SHIT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 DBT -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-yellow">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">06 / DBT</p>

      <h2>
        SURVIVE<br>
        THE FUCKING<br>
        MOMENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Sometimes you don't need a profound explanation
        of your childhood at 9:47 PM.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes you need to get through the next twenty minutes
        without making your life harder.
      </p>

      <p>
        DBT brings acceptance and change together.
      </p>

      <p>
        The craving can be real.
        The pain can be real.
        The urge to escape can make complete sense.
      </p>

      <p>
        And you can still choose not to act on it.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-lens">
        <span>DBT ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT SKILL HELPS ME GET THROUGH THIS MOMENT WITHOUT MAKING IT WORSE?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <blockquote>
        THIS FUCKING SUCKS.<br>
        AND I STILL DON'T HAVE TO MAKE IT WORSE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 IFS -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-purple">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">07 / IFS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IS<br>
        THE ADDICTED<br>
        PART DOING?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        IFS offers a different question than
        "Why the fuck do I keep doing this?"
      </p>

      <p>
        It asks what the part of you reaching for the substance
        or behavior is trying to accomplish.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe it wants numbness.
        Maybe it wants excitement.
        Maybe it wants sleep.
        Maybe it wants mania to keep going.
        Maybe it wants depression to disappear.
        Maybe it wants you to stop feeling anything for five fucking minutes.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-lens">
        <span>IFS ASKS:</span>
        <h3>
          WHAT IS THIS PART TRYING TO DO FOR ME?
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p>
        Understanding the function doesn't mean handing that part
        your wallet, your keys, or control of the night.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        LISTEN TO WHAT IT NEEDS.<br>
        DON'T AUTOMATICALLY DO WHAT IT WANTS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 BIPOLAR + ADDICTION -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-black">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">08 / BIPOLAR + ADDICTION</p>

      <h2>
        CHECK<br>
        THE MOOD<br>
        TOO.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        When bipolar disorder and addiction overlap,
        the substance isn't always the whole fucking story.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania or hypomania can increase impulsivity,
        confidence, risk-taking, reward-seeking, and reduced inhibition.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression can create its own pull toward escape,
        numbness, relief, or not giving a shit what happens next.
      </p>

      <p>
        And substances can complicate mood, sleep, judgment,
        treatment, and your ability to tell what the hell is happening.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-mood-check">

        <div>
          <span>ASK ABOUT THE URGE</span>
          <p>
            What do I want right now?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ASK ABOUT THE MOOD</span>
          <p>
            What has changed in my sleep, energy, thoughts, and behavior?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ASK ABOUT THE ESCAPE</span>
          <p>
            What am I trying not to feel?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>ASK ABOUT THE CONSEQUENCE</span>
          <p>
            Where does this actually take me?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        DON'T JUST WATCH THE ADDICTION.<br>
        WATCH THE WHOLE SYSTEM.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 PUT THEM TOGETHER -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-pink">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">09 / PUT IT TOGETHER</p>

      <h2>
        SIX<br>
        QUESTIONS.<br>
        ONE URGE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Say the urge hits.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-stack">

        <div>
          <span>BUDDHISM</span>
          <p>
            Can I let this craving exist without obeying it?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>HINDUISM</span>
          <p>
            Can I remember that this state isn't all of me?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>STOICISM</span>
          <p>
            What can I actually do about this moment?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CBT</span>
          <p>
            What story is giving me permission to act?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DBT</span>
          <p>
            What gets me through this without making things worse?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>IFS</span>
          <p>
            What is the part that wants this trying to accomplish?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        You don't need to conduct a six-part philosophical conference
        every time you get a craving.
      </p>

      <p>
        You just need more than one way to respond.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-yellow">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">10 / KEEP IT IN PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2>
        TOOLS.<br>
        NOT<br>
        MAGIC.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        None of these perspectives cures bipolar disorder
        or magically eliminates addiction.
      </p>

      <p>
        They're ways to understand experiences,
        create space around urges, examine thoughts,
        tolerate distress, and make different choices.
      </p>

      <p>
        They can sit alongside psychiatric care, therapy,
        medication, addiction treatment, recovery communities,
        sobriety plans, and whatever else belongs in your recovery.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        USE WHAT HELPS.<br>
        DON'T ASK A LENS TO BE A CURE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ap-ending-small">
        SAME URGE. MORE OPTIONS.
      </p>

      <h2>
        CHANGE<br>
        THE<br>
        <span>LENS.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ap-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Notice the craving.
      </p>

      <p>
        Separate yourself from it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Figure out what's yours to do.
      </p>

      <p>
        Question the story.
      </p>

      <p>
        Survive the moment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Get curious about what you're actually trying to escape.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then choose what happens next.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ap-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>